CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Act is to prescribe matters regarding coast guard to ensure maritime security, maintain public safety and order, and protect marine fishery resources and maritime facilities, thereby contributing to the safety of citizens and the maintenance of public order. <Amended on Apr. 18, 2017>

Article 2 (Definitions) The terms used in this Act are defined as follows: <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Apr. 18, 2017; Jul. 26, 2017; Dec. 29, 2020; Apr. 22, 2025>
  1. The term "coast guard" means the exercise of police authority by the Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard to ensure maritime security and maritime public safety for safeguarding maritime sovereignty and to protect marine fishery resources and maritime facilities in guarded waters;
  2. The term "guarded waters" means the coastal waters, offshore waters, and open sea waters to which the rights of the Republic of Korea extend under the statutes and regulations of the Republic of Korea and international law;
  3. The term "coastal waters" means territorial seas and internal waters (excluding surface of internal waters under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Inland Water Fisheries Act) under Articles 1 and 3 of the Territorial Sea and Contiguous Zone Act;
  4. The term "offshore waters" means the contiguous zone referred to in Article 3-2 of the Territorial Sea and Contiguous Zone Act;
  5. The term "open sea waters" means the waters excluding the coastal waters and the offshore waters, which are referred to in subparagraph 1 of Article 3 of the Framework Act on Marine Fishery Development;
  6. The term "marine fishery resources" means marine fishery resources referred to in subparagraph 2 of Article 3 of the Framework Act on Marine Fishery Development;
  7. The term "maritime facility" means a maritime facility under subparagraph 17 of Article 2 of the Marine Environment Management Act;
  8. The term "guard force" means the manpower, naval vessels, aircraft, and telecommunication equipment mobilized by the Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard for coast guard;
  9. The term "maritime inspection and search" means an act against vessels, etc. within the guarded waters, whereby the Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard, using guard forces, demands that vessels stop, boards vessels, conducts questioning, verifies facts, searches hulls, or takes other necessary measures;
  10. The term "vessels, etc." means a vessel under Article 1-2 (1) of the Ship Act (hereinafter referred to as "vessel"), a water leisure craft under subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Water-Related Leisure Activities Safety Act, a fishing vessel under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Fishing Vessels Act, and other craft movable with persons boarding;
  11. The term "critical coastal facilities" means major industrial facilities adjacent to the sea that are essential to the national economy, such as public institutions, airports, ports, power plants, shipyards, and oil storage facilities, as prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  12. The term “coast guard information” means information within the guarded waters necessary for coast guard activities, which corresponds to any of the following:
  a. Information on guarded waters and guard forces, etc.;
  b. Information on the operation and security and safety of vessels, etc. (including information on the location of vessels, entry and departure, crew members, cargo, etc.);
  c. Information on the security and safety of maritime facilities, critical coastal facilities, etc.;
  d. Information collected at sea in the form of images, audio, acoustic signals, or telecommunications data, or information processed, refined, or utilized from a combination of such data;
  e. Information on marine characteristics, such as water depth, tidal currents, and marine meteorology, and on seabed resources, marine fishery resources, etc.;
  f. Other information prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  g. Information generated by combining, integrating, analyzing, or processing the information specified in items a through f.

Article 3 (Responsibilities of the State) The State shall establish a system and prepare conditions necessary for coast guard and formulate and implement policies therefor to ensure maritime security and public safety and to protect marine fishery resources and maritime facilities in the guarded waters. <Amended on Apr. 18, 2017>

Article 4 (Scope of application) This Act shall apply to any of the following vessels, etc. or maritime facilities:
  1. Vessels, etc. or maritime facility located in the guarded waters;
  2. Vessels of the Republic of Korea under Article 2 of the Ship Act located in any waters other than the guarded waters.

Article 5 (Relationship to other statutes) (1) Except as provided in the United Defense Act in relation to coast guard, this Act shall apply.
  (2) Except as provided in this Act in relation to coast guard, the Act on the Performance of Duties by Police Officers shall apply. <Amended on May 20, 2014>

Article 5-2 Deleted. <Aug. 20, 2019>

CHAPTER II COAST GUARD ACTIVITIES

Article 6 (Formulation of master plans for coast guard) (1) The Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard shall formulate and implement a master plan for coast guard (hereinafter referred to as "master plan") every 5 years to conduct efficient coast guard activities. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  (2) A master plan shall include the following matters:
  1. Matters regarding the analysis of maritime public safety needs in response to changes in the surrounding circumstances;
  2. Matters regarding operation plans for guard forces and international cooperation based on maritime public safety needs;
  3. Matters concerning prospects for increase and decrease of guard force, and securing manpower and financial resources therefor;
  4. Matters concerning guard methods suitable for the characteristics of each guarded water;
  5. Other matters necessary for operating coast guard.
  (3) When intending to formulate a master plan, the Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard shall hear the opinions of the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies such as the Minister of Foreign Affairs, the Minister of National Defense, and the Commissioner General of the Korean National Police Agency, and the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Do Governor, and a Special Self-Governing Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as "Mayor/Do Governor"). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017; Dec. 29, 2020>
  (4) The Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard shall formulate an annual coast guard plan containing the objects that need to be heavily guarded, achievement goals, etc. after analyzing the actual results of coast guard activities of the previous year and the conditions of public safety, etc., in accordance with the formulated master plan. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 7 (Scope of coast guard activities) A police official under the jurisdiction of the Korea Coast Guard (hereinafter referred to as "coast guard police officer") shall conduct any of the following coast guard activities: <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Apr. 18, 2017; Jul. 26, 2017>
  1. Prevention of maritime-related crimes;
  2. Measures for the control of marine pollution and the protection of marine fishery resources;
  3. Execution of maritime security, counter-terrorism, and counter-espionage operations;
  4. Measures for the protection of maritime facilities;
  5. Measures for the protection of maritime navigation;
  6. Maintenance of public safety and order for coast guard in the guarded waters.

Article 7-2 (Collection of coast guard information) (1) The Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard may collect, manage, and utilize coast guard information necessary for coast guard activities.
  (2) When collecting coast guard information under paragraph (1), the Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard shall collect only the minimum information necessary for the intended purpose and must not use the collected information for any purpose other than the intended purpose.
  (3) The Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard may establish and operate a coast guard information system to collect, manage, and analyze coast guard information.
  (4) The Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard may entrust part of the tasks related to the establishment and operation of the coast guard information system under paragraph (3) to a relevant specialized institution or organization, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, if specialized technical expertise is required.
  (5) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (4), detailed matters necessary for the collection, management, etc. of coast guard information shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Apr. 22, 2025]

Article 8 (Prohibition of abuse of authority) When coast guard police officers perform their duties under this Act, they shall not infringe the rights and freedom of individuals by abusing their authority. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 9 (International cooperation) (1) The Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard may dispatch a part of guard forces abroad for joint inter-state training and relief activities, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017; Dec. 3, 2019>
  (2) The Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard may transfer a decommissioned patrol ship free of charge to a developing country defined in subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Framework Act on International Development Cooperation to promote international cooperation, notwithstanding Article 55 of the State Property Act. <Added on Dec. 3, 2019>
  (3) Criteria and methods of selecting developing countries eligible for transfer under paragraph (2) and detailed procedures for transfer shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Added on Dec. 3, 2019>
  [Title Amended on Dec. 3, 2019]

Article 10 (Establishment and operation of consultative body) (1) The Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard may establish and operate a consultative body jointly with the relevant agencies, such as the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, and the Korean National Police Agency, for business cooperation, such as prompt collection and dissemination of information, in case of emergency related to coast guard activities. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  (2) Matters necessary for the establishment, operation, etc. of the consultative body under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 11 (Priority guard matters for each guarded water) (1) The Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard may deploy guard force and put priority on guard differently according to the classification of the guarded waters. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  (2) Priority guard matters based on the classification referred to in paragraph (1) shall be as follows: <Amended on Apr. 18, 2017>
  1. Coastal waters: Ensuring public security and protecting and guarding critical coastal facilities, involving control of vessels, etc. violating any maritime-related domestic statute or regulation;
  2. Offshore waters: Guard for controlling foreign vessels violating the statutes and regulations under Article 6-2 of the Territorial Sea and Contiguous Zone Act;
  3. Open sea waters: Guard for controlling foreign vessels violating any domestic statute or regulation or any treaty concluded and ratified by the Republic of Korea, regarding the protection of marine fishery resources and maritime facilities, the conservation and management of the marine environment, and the conduct of marine scientific research.

Article 12 (Maritime inspection and search) (1) A coast guard police officer may conduct maritime inspection and search of any of the following vessels, etc., if there are considerable reasons judging from circumstances; provided, the maritime inspection and search of a foreign vessel shall be conducted in compliance with the treaties concluded and ratified by the Republic of Korea or generally recognized international law: <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017; Apr. 22, 2025>
  1. Vessels, etc. which impede the safe navigation of other vessels, or are operated in deviation from normal navigation without consistent navigational status, such as the route;
  2. Vessels, etc. suspected of being used to transport weapons of mass destruction, other weapons, or materials related thereto;
  2-2. Vessels, etc. suspected of being used to transport prohibited goods designated and publicly notified by the Minister of Trade, Industry and Energy to make a special contribution to international efforts to maintain international peace and security;
  3. Vessels, etc. suspected of violating any domestic statute or regulation or any treaty concluded and ratified by the Republic of Korea, or of being about to commit such violations.
  (2) If a coast guard police officer boards a vessel, etc. for the purpose of conducting maritime inspection and search, the officer shall inform the captain of such vessel, etc. (including the person operating such vessel; hereinafter the same shall apply) of his or her affiliation, name, and purpose and grounds of such maritime inspection and search. <Amended on Aug. 13, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 13 (Pursuit and seizure) A coast guard police officer may pursue and seize any of the following vessels, etc.; provided, Article 111 of the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea shall apply to the exercise of pursuit of foreign vessels: <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  1. Vessels, etc., which flee without complying with maritime inspection and search under Article 12;
  2. Vessels, etc., which are reasonably believed to have violated or which are about to violate any domestic statute or regulation or any treaty concluded and ratified by the Republic of Korea, which governs the relevant guarded waters.

Article 14 (Protective measures for maritime navigation) (1) A coast guard police officer may take a protective measure for maritime navigation, such as issuing a warning or an order to move or disperse, against the captains of vessels, etc. which commit any of the following acts; provided, protective measures for maritime navigation against a foreign vessel may be taken only in coastal waters: <Amended on Aug. 13, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  1. Acts by vessels, etc. of significantly impeding the navigation, arrival, or departure of other vessels, etc. beyond their original purposes;
  2. Acts by vessels, etc. of occupying any waters inside or outside of a harbor or port or any designated route in a group for a long time, or acts by vessels, etc. of impeding the navigation of other vessels, etc. by deviating from normal crossing methods in navigation;
  3. Acts by vessels, etc. of navigating or occupying the guarded waters within 1 kilometer outward from the boundary of any critical coastal facility in a manner demonstrating their power, which are highly likely to cause a safety-related accident.
  (2) If harm to human lives and bodies, serious property loss, or marine pollution is highly likely to occur because vessels, etc. are in danger of stranding, collision, sinking, or destruction due to any of the following reasons in guarded waters (excluding the water zone of a trade port under the Act on the Arrival and Departure of Ships, in this paragraph), a coast guard police officer may take safety measures against the captain of that vessels, etc. such as issuing a warning and an order to relocate or evacuate; provided, safety measures for a foreign vessel may be taken only in coastal waters: <Added on Aug. 13, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Feb. 3, 2015; Jul. 26, 2017>
  1. Natural disasters, such as typhoons and tsunamis;
  2. Explosion of dangerous substances or fire on the vessel;
  3. Destruction of maritime structures.
  (3) If a coast guard police officer is unable to issue an order under paragraph (2) due to breakdown, etc. of communication devices of vessels, etc. or if the captains of vessels, etc. refuse to comply with an order issued under paragraph (2), the officer may take the following measures to the extent reasonably deemed necessary, if such measures are deemed urgent or necessary to prevent harm to human lives and bodies, serious property loss, or marine pollution: <Added on Aug. 13, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  1. Measure to relocate vessels, etc. to a safe place;
  2. Measure to require the captain, sailors, or passengers of vessels, etc. to disembark to evacuate them to a safe place;
  3. Other measures prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (4) If a coast guard police officer intends to take measures under paragraph (3), the officer shall present identification verifying his or her status to the captains of vessels, etc. and inform them of the purpose and reasons of the measures and the place of relocation or evacuation; provided, if the officer is unable to board the vessels, etc. due to meteorological conditions, etc., the officer may inform his or her status, etc. by means of radio communications, etc. <Added on Aug. 13, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  (5) The chief of a coast guard station may require the owners of vessels, etc. prescribed by Presidential Decree to bear the expenses incurred in taking relocation measures under paragraph (3) 1. <Added on Aug. 13, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  (6) Matters necessary for protective measures for maritime navigation, etc. under paragraphs (1) through (4) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Aug. 13, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  [Title Amended on Aug. 13, 2013]

Article 15 (Request for support) (1) Where urgent support is needed while conducting coast guard activities, the chief of a coast guard agency may request a nearby administrative agency for support, such as offering a vessel or an aircraft. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  (2) Upon receipt of a request for support under paragraph (1), the head of an administrative agency shall comply therewith unless there is good cause.

Article 16 (Education and training for coast guard) The Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard may, to smoothly perform coast guard, conduct education and training for guard personnel, such as patrol ship crews and aviation personnel, and comprehensive training by using patrol ships and aircraft. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 16-2 (Formulation of maritime counter-terrorism plans) (1) The Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard shall formulate a maritime counter-terrorism plan every 5 years for the execution of counter-terrorism operations under subparagraph 3 of Article 7, the smooth formulation of terrorism prevention measures under Article 10 of the Act on Counter-Terrorism for the Protection of Citizens and Public Security, and the efficient prevention of and response to terrorism at sea.
  (2) The chief of a coast guard agency shall formulate and implement an action plan to prevent and respond to maritime terrorism through consultation with related agencies each year in order to efficiently implement a maritime counter-terrorism plan under paragraph (1).
  (3) Matters necessary for the formulation and implementation of a maritime counter-terrorism plan under paragraph (1) and an action plan to prevent and respond to maritime terrorism under paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 29, 2020]

CHAPTER III USE OF WEAPONS AND EQUIPMENT

Article 17 (Use of weapons) (1) A coast guard police officer may use weapons in the course of conducting any of the following coast guard activities; in such case, Article 10-4 of the Act on the Performance of Duties by Police Officers shall apply to the standards for use of weapons: <Amended on May 20, 2014; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  1. For seizing vessels, etc. and arresting an offender;
  2. For thwarting the fleet of vessels, etc. or an offender;
  3. For preventing harm to the life and body of himself or herself or other persons;
  4. For suppressing resistance to the execution of public duties.
  (2) In any of the following cases, a coast guard police officer may use service firearms, in addition to personal firearms: <Amended on Apr. 18, 2017>
  1. In cases of conducting operations related to national security, such as counter-espionage operations and counter-terrorism operations;
  2. In cases specified in any of the subparagraphs of paragraph (1), where vessels, etc. and the offenders attack or intend to attack the guard force by using the hull of a vessel, or any other dangerous or deadly weapon or instrument;
  3. In cases where vessels, etc. refuse to comply with an order to stop or relocate at least 3 times, and cause or intend to inflict, in a group, danger and harm to the guard force.

Article 18 (Use of maritime police equipment and gear) (1) A coast guard police officer may use any of the following police equipment and police gear, in addition to police equipment and police gear under Articles 10 (2) and 10-2 (2) of the Act on the Performance of Duties by Police Officers: <Amended on May 20, 2014; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  1. Police equipment and police gear belonging to and operated by the guard force in cases of compulsorily stopping, blocking, or searching vessels, etc. at the time of maritime inspection and search or pursuit and seizure;
  2. Police equipment and police gear necessary for protective measures for maritime navigation, such as orders to relocate or disperse vessels, etc.;
  3. Police equipment and police gear for self-defense of the guard force where vessels, etc. unjustifiably resist, or attempt to inflict danger and harm to, the guard force during the coast guard police officers’ performance of duties, in addition to police equipment and police gear under subparagraphs 1 and 2.
  (2) The types of police equipment and police gear under paragraph (1) and the standards for their use shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [Title Amended on Jul. 26, 2017]

CHAPTER IV SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 19 (Request for cooperation) If necessary for conducting coast guard activities under subparagraphs 1 through 5 of Article 7, the Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard may request the heads of relevant administrative agencies for cooperation, such as provision of information. In such cases, the heads of relevant administrative agencies so requested shall comply with such request unless there is a compelling reason not to do so. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017; Apr. 22, 2025>

Article 20 (Notification of permission for occupancy or use within the guarded waters) (1) When the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Do Governor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu (the head of a Gu refers to the head of an autonomous Gu; hereinafter the same shall apply) grants permission for occupancy or use of public waters under Article 8 of the Public Waters Management and Reclamation Act within the guarded waters, such authority shall notify such fact to the Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard, the Commissioner of a Regional Korea Coast Guard, or the chief of the competent coast guard station if the permission is deemed to seriously impede coast guard activities specified in subparagraphs 1 through 5 of Article 7. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013, Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017; Dec. 29, 2020>
  (2) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries implements a harbor development project under subparagraph 6 Article 2 of the Harbor Act, and if such project is deemed to seriously impede coast guard activities specified in subparagraphs 1 through 5 of Article 7, the Minister shall notify such fact to the Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard, the commander of a regional coast guard or the chief of the competent coast guard station. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017; Jan. 29, 2020>
  (3) When the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, a Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu implements a fishery harbor development project under Article 23 (1) of the Fishing Villages and Fishery Harbors Act, such authority shall notify such fact to the Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard, the Commissioner of a Regional Korea Coast Guard, or the chief of the competent coast guard station if the project is deemed to seriously impede coast guard activities under subparagraphs 1 through 5 of Article 7. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>
  (4) Where a Mayor/Do Governor grants a fishery business license under Article 7 of the Fisheries Act or a license under Article 10 of the Aquaculture Industry Development Act, the Mayor/Do Governor shall notify the chief of the competent coast guard station of matters related to coast guard activities under each subparagraph of Article 7. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017; Aug. 27, 2019; Jan. 11, 2022>
  (5) Detailed matters to be notified and procedures for notifying such matters under paragraphs (1) through (4) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Nov. 19, 2014; Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 20-2 (Awards) The Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard may grant awards to those who have rendered distinguished service in seizing a foreign vessel under Article 13, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Jul. 26, 2017>
  [This Article Added on Apr. 18, 2017]

Article 20-3 (Legal fiction as public officials for purposes of applying penalty provisions) An executive officer or employee of a specialized institution or organization engaged in entrusted tasks under Article 7-2 (4) who is not a public official shall be deemed a public official for purposes of applying Article 127 of the Criminal Act.
  [This Article Added on Apr. 22, 2025]

CHAPTER V PENALTY PROVISIONS

Article 21 (Penalty provisions) (1) A person who refuses, obstructs, or evades maritime inspection and search under Article 12 (1) without good cause shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for up to 1 year or by a fine not exceeding 10 million won. <Added on Apr. 18, 2017>
  (2) A person who refuses, obstructs, or evades an order to relocate, disperse, or evacuate under Article 14 or a measure for relocation or evacuation shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for up to 6 months or by a fine not exceeding 5 million won. <Amended on Aug. 13, 2013; Apr. 18, 2017>

Article 22 Deleted. <Apr. 18, 2017>

